  "statement": "Let $x=\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{2}$. Show that $x^{4}-10x^{2}+1=0$.",
  "solution": "Compute $x^{2}=(\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{2})^{2}=3+2+2\\sqrt{6}=5+2\\sqrt{6}$. Then $x^{4}=(5+2\\sqrt{6})^{2}=25+24+20\\sqrt{6}=49+20\\sqrt{6}$. Hence $x^{4}-10x^{2}+1=(49+20\\sqrt{6})-10(5+2\\sqrt{6})+1=49+20\\sqrt{6}-50-20\\sqrt{6}+1=0$.",
  "hints": [
    "Square $x$ once to get $x^{2}=5+2\\sqrt{6}$.",
    "Square again and substitute back."
  ],
